<% @wide = true %>
<h1>Signing the add-on</h1>

<p class="lede">
    Firefox takes an unsigned add-on for one session and drops it at the
    next restart. Signing it once makes the same download install for
    good — and publishes nothing.
</p>

<h2>What signing is, and is not</h2>

<dl class="railed">
    <dt>Not publishing</dt>
    <dd>
        An <em>unlisted</em> submission never appears on
        addons.mozilla.org, is not searchable, and has no listing page.
        Mozilla signs the package and hands it back; where it is hosted
        afterwards is your business — here, this service.
    </dd>

    <dt>Not review</dt>
    <dd>
        Unlisted submissions are checked by machine, not by a human
        reviewer, and come back in a minute or two. What comes back is
        the same package with a signature in it.
    </dd>

    <dt>Not permanent work</dt>
    <dd>
        It is one command per release. The keys are issued once and kept
        wherever this service's other secrets live.
    </dd>
</dl>

<h2>Doing it</h2>

<ol class="steps is-tall">
    <li>
        On <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/developers/addon/api/key/">addons.mozilla.org</a>,
        sign in and open <em>Tools → Manage API keys</em>. Generate a
        credential: it gives you an issuer (<code>user:1234567:890</code>)
        and a secret. The secret is shown once.
    </li>

    <li>
        On the machine that runs this service:
<pre><code>export AMO_JWT_ISSUER=user:1234567:890
export AMO_JWT_SECRET=…
cd <%= root %>
rake addon:sign</code></pre>
        It builds the Firefox package, sends it, waits, and puts the
        signed result at <code>extension/dist/firefox.xpi</code>.
    </li>

    <li>
        Nothing else to do. This service prefers a <code>.xpi</code> over
        a <code>.zip</code>, serves it as
        <code>application/x-xpi</code> — which is what makes Firefox
        offer to install rather than to save — and the
        <a href="<%= mount("/") %>#add-on">add-on box</a> starts saying
        <em>Install</em> instead of <em>Download</em>.
    </li>
</ol>

<h2>Afterwards</h2>

<dl class="railed">
    <dt>Updating</dt>
    <dd>
        <code>rake addon:version TO=0.2.0</code> writes both manifests and
        rebuilds the packages; then <code>rake addon:sign</code> again. Firefox
        does not update a self-hosted add-on by itself unless the package
        names an update URL, so whoever has it installed installs the new
        one from the same link.
    </dd>

    <dt>Its own number</dt>
    <dd>
        The add-on's version is not the service's. It is installed rather
        than served, so a browser may be carrying any build while the
        service is only ever the one it is running — and AMO wants the
        number to rise with every upload, which has nothing to do with how
        often the endpoint changes. <code>rake addon:version</code> with no
        <code>TO</code> says what the manifests claim now.
    </dd>

    <dt>The add-on id</dt>
    <dd>
        <code><%= addon_id %></code>, in
        <code>manifest.firefox.json</code>. It must not change between
        versions: to Firefox, a different id is a different add-on, and
        the signature is bound to it.
    </dd>

    <dt>Why a UUID</dt>
    <dd>
        An id may be an address or a UUID, and this one is a UUID because
        the alternative names somebody. AMO ids are unique across the
        whole store, so a <em>second</em> deployment signing its own build
        of this must mint its own — <code>uuidgen</code>, braces included
        — before the first upload, and never touch it again.
    </dd>

    <dt>Chrome</dt>
    <dd>
        No equivalent, and nothing to run. Chrome installs extensions
        from its Web Store only, so the choices are an unlisted Web
        Store listing — a one-off developer fee and a review — or
        loading the unpacked folder, which is what the box on the front
        page describes. For two or three people, unpacked is the honest
        answer.
    </dd>
</dl>

<p class="empty">
    <a href="<%= mount("/") %>#add-on">Back to the add-on</a>
</p>
